#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199320} :activity/id #uuid "342a691e-fc66-56ce-aabf-098ff3242cb2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049315 :topic/id #uuid "eba98dcb-66f5-5f67-ad46-4c466f76f5ef" :topic/math-academy-id 4162} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199296 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198390 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198032 :question/id #uuid "5ff4ed47-dffe-534e-baf9-41bded65d457" :question/math-academy-id "e-12385"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "500d2422-474b-55a3-8fe7-05799ba578af" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050188}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475984}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Rectangular Solid"} :step/id #uuid "f24da8a5-d03b-52d4-93ed-bd3587f66dfa" :step/math-academy-id 44438 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199322}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199297 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198391 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198033 :question/id #uuid "06cccfa3-4b70-5e67-9e9f-9f5c83b319bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-12386"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "315e5799-4bcc-5f2d-b30b-6a3c945e1589" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050966}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198603} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423452}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Sphere"} :step/id #uuid "bfcf599a-5392-50e6-bb70-aea6cfbb105a" :step/math-academy-id 44442 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199321}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199298 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199469 :tutorial/content "![](images/d2/d2a5641e502b11cca4f469ec743adc62d614f669d61c1b2d0b0c7549fb60bf6b.png)\n\n \n\nGiven a sphere of radius $r$ centered at the origin, an outward-pointing unit normal vector to the sphere at the point $P(x,y,z)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can sometimes use this to calculate the flux of a vector field through a sphere. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "6dc7d279-b10e-5ca3-b496-0382ff5c50ac" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7652 :tutorial/title "The Outward-Pointing Unit Vector to a Sphere"} :step/id #uuid "2deaec25-cf1d-52dd-97eb-efa9bad2cd8f" :step/math-academy-id 44445 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199297}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199320 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199479 :tutorial/content "Similar to the case of *closed* curves, there is always a well-defined notion of \"inside\" and \"outside\" a closed *surface*. \n\nFor closed surfaces, the convention is the positive orientation is always given by the *outward-pointing* unit normal vector.\n\n![](images/d2/d2bb07e4684cfc3a8c5641ecc6930dbc1c76027a735c7efe0c1c60cd17a91060.png)\n\n \n\nWhen calculating the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf F$ across a closed surface, we always measure the flux relative to the surface's positive orientation.  \n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "9a27f82e-a934-5e25-87cc-8140ba349eae" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7726 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "70493dbd-ab3f-5404-8591-79605bde33e1" :step/math-academy-id 44783 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199296}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199321 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199480 :tutorial/content "Let's derive the formula for a unit normal vector for a sphere of radius $r$ centered at the origin. \n\nConsider the function  \n\n$$\nw = f (x, y, z)= \\dfrac 1 2\\left(x^2 + y^2 + z^2 - r^2\\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nThe level surface corresponding to $w=0$ is the sphere $S$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = r^2.$ \n\nConsider the point $P(x,y,z)$ that lies on $S.$ Then, $\\nabla f(x,y,z)$ is a normal vector to the level surface at the point $P$.\n\nComputing $\\nabla f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨{f}_{x},\\,{f}_{y},\\,{f}_{z}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,2z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $P(x,y,z)$ lies on $S,$ we have $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = r^2.$\nNormalizing $\\nabla f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{∇f(x,y,z)}{‖∇f(x,y,z)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit normal vector for a sphere of radius $r$ centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "51218e5e-009c-5c0c-8034-3440e9b736c0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7729 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Unit Normal Vector for a Sphere"} :step/id #uuid "c5b99d56-2b0a-5e62-9a25-4853c59d5bab" :step/math-academy-id 44787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199322 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198404 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198046 :question/id #uuid "8d046f27-886d-5342-8868-e2c3ae731fa5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12453"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d07bcae4-cae8-5eb0-a93f-798ba20485a6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049084}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423453}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Cylinder"} :step/id #uuid "7203bf6c-a682-5850-807b-ad02403f986a" :step/math-academy-id 44792 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199298}}] :activity/title "Flux Through Closed Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198493}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475999} :activity/id #uuid "41a71fe0-482d-55e4-85d0-de94d279d70a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049315 :topic/id #uuid "eba98dcb-66f5-5f67-ad46-4c466f76f5ef" :topic/math-academy-id 4162} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475987 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0de413f9-cd7a-535a-8ffa-46d5c7da302d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475463} {:answer/id #uuid "2a020e56-2589-5e05-8a71-55eaf678b2b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475498} {:answer/id #uuid "5d0e7c8f-f911-5f64-b0c6-19e7a9add138" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475578} {:answer/id #uuid "720df5c2-6cd7-54e9-a9af-ea4b64405c63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475604} {:answer/id #uuid "7c27cb5a-7606-5667-9a19-07de7ad9160b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475621}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475463} :answer-field/id #uuid "2652e0ca-544c-51d5-827f-9347b3d96ac0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fd38a4fc-c1e2-5044-998c-eee65f3245ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-162559" :question/problem "![](images/80/804976bf326ed561ff8e7b4f5a6c65361039a686a4449edfdf8e6f497ee5cbda.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,xy-z,\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $y$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,xy-z,\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $y$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the right and left sides. \n\n![](images/de/de9eb8381df05d2d5e6222a51b0ccbc5729e522df94a4aace570881d1226a1ca.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the right surface. On this surface, we have $y=1$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le z\\le 2.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=1$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,xy-z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,x-z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =x-z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}x-z\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x-z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x-z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-xz]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}-z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}z-\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the left surface. On this surface, we have $y=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le z\\le 2.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,xy-z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}z\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[x]_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =(-1)+2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "01b6cab6-7e9c-559f-bbcf-44a96f585fe0"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475989 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278432 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71b4b278-b65f-5406-9647-519faf867137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276501} {:answer/id #uuid "7305c0b2-d8c2-5e47-bbb3-3425beae0e64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276519} {:answer/id #uuid "9eb283a3-ac7c-5ffa-9921-e523b36374a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276903} {:answer/id #uuid "bca4ccd7-be58-541f-ae27-b6666d1303c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277157} {:answer/id #uuid "be2e5824-d10f-5cc6-81d8-eaf9f7d77d64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277175}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276501} :answer-field/id #uuid "d99b0f37-11a6-51ae-b457-b0354976d304" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8c3f4c19-765d-5833-904a-bf7538da3049" :question/math-academy-id "q-161222" :question/problem "![](images/01/01902836fd1e91ac829f67a279103a9b3d5732d7b058f03c695b6733a42e673d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+z⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/c0/c058465d6ca7855446711ebc44f6b23913ee786d2fd9bb32ae6c671964e1ad32.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=1$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=1$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+x{y}^{2}+x]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\frac{5}{4}y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-({x}^{3}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{12}+(-\\frac{7}{12}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "17afeff9-39f1-5994-86da-13a9c620fd75" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475987}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475993 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22aa9306-b821-5f7a-bcc1-13baa3e5f288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{64\\pi (8-3\\sqrt{3})}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275787} {:answer/id #uuid "6643cf61-097c-51f6-b17b-d1aa26ebd1a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi +3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276393} {:answer/id #uuid "86d98f42-d1f5-5fa9-b04c-8d2af7e4a2cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27\\pi (4-\\sqrt{2})}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276689} {:answer/id #uuid "8a0b4ac1-ec6a-5fd6-aac1-0b33d91f5d3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi +3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276725} {:answer/id #uuid "ba1fe40a-9855-5b61-942f-7ed5bbdea556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277126}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275787} :answer-field/id #uuid "723b6093-df88-5ee2-b562-a30395cdbb69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f41018fb-b7c4-59de-93b6-b0fee9601c2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161207" :question/problem "![](images/4c/4cb2c8128f24adddacd82b5f477848faa47ae52f95a5bb0636a8b286a6628a08.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{z}^{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{z}^{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/e6/e6fe01196298c1da0961fd01c73ee4cb7c71b6a03a027cceaba0e68be944c968.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=2$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{z}^{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{2}^{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}4\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =4{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =4{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=16-{r}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=-2r\\,\\text{d}r.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & 0 & 2 \\\\ u & 16 & 12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r & =8\\pi \\cdot {\\int }_{16}^{12}\\sqrt{u}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{12}^{16}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\pi \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{12}^{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}[64-24\\sqrt{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\pi (8-3\\sqrt{3})}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{0}^{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\pi (8-3\\sqrt{3})}{3}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\pi (8-3\\sqrt{3})}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "58a9eb2b-d540-5d6b-9d55-2b38cf818ec8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476000}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475999 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198601 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "310404f5-f868-54f7-b01b-a2a18674d442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275913} {:answer/id #uuid "92f872ee-006f-5878-8d2d-24cd0725ec4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276809} {:answer/id #uuid "ac8126eb-6bfa-51f0-a391-c2f103b6f6a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277027} {:answer/id #uuid "d62e99f6-a80a-5c52-82a2-08949d00886a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277362} {:answer/id #uuid "d9bb63e7-16d0-505d-be1f-49a3b1cdc699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277388}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275913} :answer-field/id #uuid "2af4217f-1686-5f6d-be11-0fc1f94ca999" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "300078ae-d2f8-55e0-bd83-9b0b9c3615b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-161162" :question/problem "![](images/89/8901e7155a476f0019b6d66e3b62b579b91daf99a448e497c93efce1d94415b0.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+yz⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+yz⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/8f/8fa71e16d84eb92506e9b10269182f907cb4f2437d0a9bdd3822da48c4cbba4a.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+yz⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+2y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =2x+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}{S}_{1} & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}2x+2y\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x+2y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x+2y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}+2xy]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1+2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[y+{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+yz⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}{S}_{2} & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-2x\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-2x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}-2x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {[{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2+(-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dbec28a3-ebf2-534e-a733-aa0d3d96980d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475993}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476000 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278431 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e9c78dc-55fa-58d4-a093-f9544e5dfbd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{28}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275601} {:answer/id #uuid "78632b46-36da-5697-b93d-bc2adc1c9045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276568} {:answer/id #uuid "79740243-a948-508f-ad95-f7961fb345fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276581} {:answer/id #uuid "7c58ab40-2a38-5ea8-a5ef-7548298d0ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276612} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6fd983-c520-5566-9c80-71e44ea0a60f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276613}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276612} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e7ff9e2-fb54-58a9-9896-204ca397e982" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "df8892a4-0ff3-5c81-818d-9a1c55b02265" :question/math-academy-id "q-161219" :question/problem "![](images/9a/9afc7f79f4612ce579e82a6b3c5086922d14051978907bfeb5c133a3f99791c7.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+z⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/fa/fa4986b1de44fa4ccc75114e64abfd93d0de16618f2f1b29695d9115019b6e3e.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=4$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=4$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =x{y}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}x{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4x]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{y}^{2}+8\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}+8y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-x{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}+(-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "edcfd322-cba5-5279-917b-33694a5197bb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475989}}] :activity/title "Flux Through Closed Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475439}]}
